
[38;5;231m⏺[39m One more structural question, then I'll present the design.

  [1mThe prerequisite problem:[0m you asked for notifications, but notifications need tasks that exist, have due dates, and
  persist across a page load — otherwise there's nothing to notify about and no "while you were gone" to catch up on.
  None of that exists. So some amount of task app has to get built regardless; the only question is how we package it.

  [1mThe fork:[0m how to sequence the prerequisite.

  - [1mOne spec, task layer scoped to the minimum[0m — add/complete/delete, a due date, localStorage persistence, and the
    notification subsystem on top. The task layer is deliberately thin: just enough to make notifications real and
    testable. Risk: the thin task layer tempts scope creep.
  - [1mTwo specs, task foundation first[0m — a proper task app, then notifications as a second cycle. Cleaner separation, but
    it delays the thing you actually asked for and invites over-designing a task app you didn't request.
  - [1mNotifications against seeded fixtures[0m — no CRUD UI at all; a hardcoded task list exercises the subsystem. Fastest to
    the actual ask, but the app stays unusable and the catch-up path can't be demonstrated honestly.

  [1mMy recommendation: one spec, task layer scoped to the minimum.[0m Notifications are the deliverable; the task layer is
  scaffolding for it, and writing a separate spec for scaffolding costs a full cycle to describe a to-do list. I'll
  state the task layer's boundary explicitly in the spec — no editing, no tags, no priorities, no sorting — so "minimum"
  is enforceable rather than aspirational.
